WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.140
- My mom gave birth to me,

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.730
and they handed me a banh mi right away.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:03.750
It's a lifestyle.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.499
The girls that get it, get it.

00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:08.790
(intro music splash)

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:09.623
What's up, everyone?

00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:12.690
My name is Tway, and
welcome to "Making It Big",

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.930
the show where I turn
your favorite foods giant.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.840
Today, I have my friend
Marco in the studio with me,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.420
and I think he has a
challenge up his sleeve.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.980
So let's see what he got.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:23.940
- Hey, Tway.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.170
- What's up?

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.150
- Well, I have something underneath here.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.830
You and I have done once before,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.050
but have not, you know, made it big.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.130
I challenge you to make
the Mama Dough Banh Mi.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.160
- Ooh, this is the Mama Dough Banh Mi.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.620
It's named after my mom, Mama Dough,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.150
because this is her recipe.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.580
The nem, the cured pork in here is hers.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.400
And it was a collaboration
between Open Market

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.479
and I, and we fed over how many people?

00:00:50.479 --> 00:00:54.090
- [Marco] 250? When we first
did it, we were playing around

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.760
with two ideas and they
were like, that tastes good.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.780
This tastes good.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:58.613
Let's do this.

00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:00.600
You're like, oh my gosh,
like I have this recipe.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.920
My mom's let me bring this in,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.530
the pate, the pa-Tway,
as we, as we called it

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.110
when we first made it, that's
a nice little key element.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.397
And we tried it and I
was like, this is it.

00:01:08.397 --> 00:01:09.780
- And then we birthed the Mama Dough.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.770
- Yeah.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.720
- This sandwich uses your pate.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.791
I'm gonna need a lot of your pate.

00:01:14.791 --> 00:01:15.624
- How much?

00:01:15.624 --> 00:01:16.457
- Like 10.

00:01:16.457 --> 00:01:17.490
- 10 quarts?

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.323
- Yeah.

00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:19.156
- I'll make it happen.

00:01:19.156 --> 00:01:20.700
- Let's get started, I had to change

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.570
into this shirt because
this is the exclusive

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.030
colab tee between Open Market and I,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.450
this is a collage of
my mom, had to wear it.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.090
Let's get started on a bread,
which I'm really nervous about

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.240
because I actually didn't
make the bread for the colab.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.350
So, the fact that I have
to make a big, giant one?

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.490
We're gonna see, I'm gonna start

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.000
off by blooming my yeast
in water right here.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.530
Give it a little stir.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.400
Let that sit.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.070
10 and a half cups of flour.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.720
And this is only half.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.250
This is so much flour.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.000
And then, and then salt it.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.554
You're gonna have to use a lot of salt.

00:01:58.554 --> 00:01:59.640
I'm gonna whisk this up.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.990
And then my starter,

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.930
this has been starting since last night.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.780
It's just yeast, flour, and water.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.750
And then add the yeast, I don't specialize

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.920
in bread making because that's
just not what I do, okay?

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.250
I had to take a bread making
class in culinary school.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.590
There was one day where I made literally

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.660
the most perfect baguette.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.882
I got a perfect score, so.

00:02:23.882 --> 00:02:24.715
(sad piano music)

00:02:24.715 --> 00:02:25.548
And then after that,

00:02:25.548 --> 00:02:27.360
I never got another perfect
score, but that's okay.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.550
Really glad that I didn't
go to the gym today

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.010
because I'm about to get a workout in.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.800
So the dough is getting under my nails

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.790
and I'm kind of regretting
the decision to have nails.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.840
It's a lifestyle.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.610
The girls that get it, get it.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.200
I was born in Vietnam.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.200
I don't really remember the
first time I've had a banh mi.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.580
My mom gave birth to me and they

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.350
handed me a banh mi right away, so.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.420
She's looking gorgeous.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.960
Now I'm about to transfer
this big boy into this bowl.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.253
Gonna grease it first.

00:02:57.253 --> 00:03:00.420
(metal bowl clanging)

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.080
Okay, more grease, cover.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.600
And then now we're
gonna let our dough baby

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.910
take a nap for about 90 minutes.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.270
90 minutes is the perfect nap time.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.110
Don't quote me on that though,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.210
I just saw it on the internet.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:16.950
And the baby has risen.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.240
Wow.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.223
It grew so much.

00:03:20.223 --> 00:03:22.020
This is so much dough.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.480
Okay, I gotta get the air bubbles out,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.520
but I have not made a bread this big.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.070
So I'm really kind of
nervous about how this is

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:29.903
going to turn out.

00:03:29.903 --> 00:03:32.310
I just don't know, but I am doing my best.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.637
Reminder, this is just
half of the banh mi.

00:03:35.637 --> 00:03:38.090
All right, so now I'm going to fold it

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.280
in the middle, and another rule.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.870
Oh, did you see that?

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.520
I'm a bread maker.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.070
And then I'm gonna pinch it together.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:52.070
I just gotta transfer this
into this baking tray.

00:03:52.714 --> 00:03:56.340
(dough slapping counter top)

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.507
Look at this.

00:03:57.507 --> 00:03:59.730
I'm really proud of myself.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.730
I'm gonna spray and then cover it.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.930
And then I'm gonna let this baby

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.240
take a nap for about 45 minutes.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.870
So far, so good, because look at this.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.850
I am very, very proud of this.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.860
Now, we gonna make slashes.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.260
I'm not a bread maker, so, don't judge me.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.820
3, 2, 10 slashes, exactly.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.960
Now we go to the oven, in you go

00:04:24.064 --> 00:04:25.200
(oven door shutting)

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.722
Good luck.

00:04:26.722 --> 00:04:29.943
Oh my goodness.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:34.923
I'm so happy.

00:04:34.923 --> 00:04:37.260
Now, one of the component

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.500
in the sandwich is pickle
daikon and carrots.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.440
Pickle daikon and carrots is
a classic Vietnamese pickle.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.480
You will see it literally everywhere.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.330
So, I'm going to salt the veggies first.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.060
Now, salting the veggies for
pickling is really important.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.310
So it can release the liquids

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.710
and that will make your pickle crunchy.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.030
Give this a toss.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.750
So, the cuts on these veggies
are a little bit bigger,

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.030
well, a lot bigger than normal, regular

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.030
Julian cuts because
we're making it to scale.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.000
This is Making It Big,
so we're making it big.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.640
I'm gonna throw this in a strainer.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.920
So when the water pulls
out from the veggies,

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.360
it can just drop to the bottom

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.670
of the bowl and it's not
sitting in its own juices.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.850
Now we're gonna let this
sit for like five minutes

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.940
so the water can drain to
the bottom of the bowl.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.100
I'm going to rinse this off in the sink

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.720
so the veggies aren't too salty.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.260
I'm gonna transfer the veggies into this.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.990
And now I'm gonna make
the pickling liquid.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.060
It's just vinegar, hot water.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.340
Now, I like to use hot
water because I'm about to

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.200
put in some sugar and that's really

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.910
going to help dissolve the sugar.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.920
Our sugar right here.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:44.753
Whoa.

00:05:44.753 --> 00:05:46.140
(sugar splashing into water)
(laughter)

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.260
And then a little bit
of salt, whisk this up,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.980
and then I'm just gonna pour
it right into the veggies.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.500
And this can sit for 30 minutes,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.390
the longer it sits, the better it is,

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.740
cover it, let it sit.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.950
And now this is the star,
the cured pork sausage.

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.270
This is my mom's recipe.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.550
So it's very special to me.

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.880
I never got to make it with
her in the kitchen because

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.800
she never let me in her kitchen,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.750
but she gave me the recipe, so.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.970
I have some pork.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.700
I have some pork back, pork back fat.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.230
Why can't I say?

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.210
I have some boiling water and I'm going to

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.010
add some pork back fat in.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.390
While we're waiting for this to cook,

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.630
I wanna introduce you guys to
my little friend over here.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.760
Her name is a Tiny Tway and

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.630
she told me some really
interesting facts about banh mis.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.590
Did you know the word banh mi
translates to "wheat bread"?

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.020
In Vietnam, we literally just used banh mi

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:41.670
as a way to describe bread.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.730
I'm not sure where the
wheat bread is coming from

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.880
but I'll give Tiny Tway that one.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.773
This is now cooked and I'm going
to transfer it onto a tray.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.830
Oh, the smell, can't lie to you,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.990
I will never get used to the smell.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.016
Oh no.

00:06:59.016 --> 00:06:59.970
(giggling)

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.880
And then we're gonna cool
this down to room temperature.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.760
So it's time to put this bad boy together.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.070
So this is the cooled pork back fat.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:12.733
And I'm going to just add
it into my food processor.

00:07:12.733 --> 00:07:15.360
(food processor whirring)

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.310
Ooh, I like this thing.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.070
This is a ground pork belly.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.410
I'm gonna add it straight into here.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:27.410
This is actually only a quarter
of one batch for one patty.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.860
We are making two patties.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.170
Then I'm gonna move
over here to my spices.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.810
I have some water and baking powder.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:37.860
Gonna mix this up.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.560
This is really going to
help tenderize the meat.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:41.940
Add this right over the top.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.640
I have fish sauce, a little bit of salt.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.280
This is sugar, white pepper.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.220
And then this is pink curing salt.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.500
This is exactly my mom's recipe.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.960
Just, just bigger.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.200
And we pulse.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.033
Come on.

00:07:59.033 --> 00:08:01.290
(food processor whirring)

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:04.440
Okay, wait, I gotta, oh no.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.501
Okay, let's try to not break things.

00:08:07.501 --> 00:08:08.764
(food processor whirring)

00:08:08.764 --> 00:08:09.597
Wow.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.510
Okay, wait, it's really coming together.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.070
I have some shallots here.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.260
I'm gonna add into the mixture.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.137
Some garlic.

00:08:17.137 --> 00:08:19.500
(food processor whirring)

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.050
This is looking so good.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.190
I think my mom would be proud.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.220
And I am very happy with this mixture.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.260
Let's make this patty.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:33.150
This is actually 16
pounds of meat right here.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:34.740
So, let's do it.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.510
And that's only one.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.380
I'm not good at math.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.713
How much is 16 plus 16?

00:08:40.135 --> 00:08:40.968
- [Producer] 32.

00:08:40.968 --> 00:08:41.820
- 32.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:42.653
Oh man.

00:08:42.653 --> 00:08:45.870
This is so much meat.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.920
I honestly did not know
what to expect when

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.660
I was challenged to do this.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.603
And I didn't realize how, oh,
there's more meat in here.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.260
I just can't wait to see it come together.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.240
So, I'm gonna pop this in the oven

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.880
and then I'm gonna start
that process all over again

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.442
because this is only the first patty.

00:09:05.442 --> 00:09:07.532
(grunting)

00:09:07.532 --> 00:09:12.532
Okay, 16 pounds of meat
in the oven for one hour.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.050
Now we're going to assemble the
lemon grass chili oil aioli.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.770
This is my special lemon grass chili oil.

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.870
I could literally eat
this by the spoonful.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:29.130
It's so good.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.920
And now I'm going to add all of this mayo.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.920
This is the best mayo, and I'm
going to add the whole bottle

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:42.620
of the best mayo to
ever exist in the world.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.730
So I'm just going to fold this in.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.010
Look at that color.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.510
Oh, gosh, so for the popup,

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.870
I also had to do a big
batch of this aioli.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:55.350
I just feel like I'm reliving
that day all over again

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.554
but it's cool because
it was a really fun day.

00:09:57.554 --> 00:09:58.387
(aioli churning)

00:09:58.387 --> 00:10:01.410
Now, that's what good aioli sounds like.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.590
This is done now, it's about time,

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.720
we get to assemble this sandwich.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.140
I'm so hyped first, we
need to cut the bread.

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:10.973
All right.

00:10:10.973 --> 00:10:12.090
I have to really make sure that

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:14.010
it's even all the way through.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:19.010
Three, two, wow, this looks so good.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.540
Smells good.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:24.360
I'm going to put the pate on the bottom.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.340
A very generous amount.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.560
Pate is very important.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.570
You need to have pate in a banh mi.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.070
If you don't have pate in a banh mi,

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.500
I don't know if it's even
considered a banh mi, I'm sorry.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.420
So pate, for those that don't know,

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:40.170
it's basically seasoned liver
that's blended down into

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:43.680
really nice, mousse like consistency.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:46.920
We get to lay our meat
on this nice bed of pate.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.140
I can't believe this is so much meat.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:52.020
I'm very happy with the char on this.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.520
This is perfect.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.170
Why are you so perfect?

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.840
Now we move on to our aioli,

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.820
making sure every single
crevice has some aioli.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.050
And then I sprinkle a little
bit of crispy shallots on here.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:10.620
This is coming out better
than I thought it would.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:11.640
I think I'm killing it.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.730
Then I'm going to put pickle
daikon and carrots on top

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:19.290
of the meat, we cut up three
big English cucumbers for this.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.540
I feel like I'm dealing cards right now.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.460
I hope Marco is gonna like this.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:26.940
I spent a lot of time
on this, so he better.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:28.380
That looks so cute.

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:31.470
And over here, we have a special herb

